module Date

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Writing a date - the segments and their separators, decided at compile time.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Timestamp)` writes the microseconds, which is the honest default for a newtype over an
   integer and is not what anybody wants to read. This is the other rendering, and it is a formatter
   on `Core/Format/`'s pattern: a record holding a reference to the `Date` and the settings to write
   it under, with a `Show` instance, so `print("{iso8601(d)}\n")` is an ordinary format hole.

   **The settings are fields and not a template string**, which is the whole design and is what
   Analysis-Library.md §3.1 means by "a template language, its parser and a formatter" being a
   text-shaped problem this module should not have. `"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"` has to be scanned at run
   time on every call, its directives dispatched one at a time, and its mistakes discovered at the
   moment the date is printed. Every setting below is a scalar or a payload-free enum, so a preset is
   a constant, the `match` on each one folds where the format is known, and a format that names a
   segment that does not exist does not exist.

   **The presets are functions rather than constants**, which is the one place this departs from what
   a reader might expect. A formatter holds a reference to its subject, so a settings record without
   one is a second type and every call would name both - `format(d, iso8601Style)` against
   `iso8601(d)`. The preset *is* the common case, so it gets the short spelling, and `dateFormat`
   underneath takes every setting by name for the formats nobody thought of.
-}

{-
   Which of the three orders the date parts go in, or no date at all.

   Three and not every permutation: these are the orders in use, and a fourth would be a format no
   reader has. `NoDate` is what makes a time-only rendering the same record as every other, rather
   than a second formatter with half the fields missing.
-}
pub data DateOrder
  = @value(0) NoDate
  | @value(1) YearMonthDay
  | @value(2) DayMonthYear
  | @value(3) MonthDayYear

-- How far down the time is written. Each step is the one before it plus a field, so a format asks
-- for a precision rather than for a set of parts - which is what every caller actually means.
pub data TimeDetail
  = @value(0) NoTime
  | @value(1) Minutes
  | @value(2) Seconds
  | @value(3) Millis
  | @value(4) Micros

-- The month as `07`, `July` or `Jul`. English only, and deliberately: a localized month name is a
-- table per language and a choice of which, which is a library this is not.
pub data MonthStyle = @value(0) MonthNumber | @value(1) MonthName | @value(2) MonthAbbrev

pub data WeekdayStyle = @value(0) NoWeekday | @value(1) WeekdayName | @value(2) WeekdayAbbrev

{-
   The zone, whose four answers are four different things a format means by one.

   `ZoneOffset` is `+0200` and `ZoneOffsetColon` is `+02:00` - RFC 3339 requires the colon and
   RFC 5322 forbids it, which is why both are here. `ZoneZulu` is `Z` for UTC and the colon form for
   anything else, which is what an ISO-8601 timestamp is normally written as. `ZoneUtcName` is the
   literal `GMT` an HTTP date ends in, and it says nothing about the offset because that format is
   only ever written at zero.
-}
pub data ZoneStyle
  = @value(0) NoZone
  | @value(1) ZoneOffset
  | @value(2) ZoneOffsetColon
  | @value(3) ZoneZulu
  | @value(4) ZoneUtcName

{-
   A date and how to write it.

   Every field is a scalar or a payload-free enum, which is not only about folding: provenance is
   field-insensitive, so an aggregate argument reaching a record that holds a reference is read as a
   root of it, and a settings *record* passed in would make every constructor here return a borrow
   rooted in the settings. See `Core/Format/Number.yana`, which states the same constraint.

   The two separators are `CodeUnit`s rather than strings for the same reason and one more: every
   separator any of these formats uses is one unit, and a zero means none - which is what the compact
   ISO form `20260829T120000Z` is, and it costs no extra field to say.
-}
pub data DateFormat {
    value: 'Date,
    order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay,
    dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
    month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber,
    weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
    between: CodeUnit = 84,
    time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
    timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58,
    zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone
}

-- Every setting by name, for a format the presets below do not cover. The defaults are ISO-8601's,
-- so what a caller writes is the difference from it rather than the whole of a format.
pub fn dateFormat(value: 'Date, order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay, dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
                  month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber, weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
                  between: CodeUnit = 84, time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
                  timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58, zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone) -> DateFormat =
    DateFormat {value: value, order: order, dateSeparator: dateSeparator, month: month,
                weekday: weekday, between: between, time: time, timeSeparator: timeSeparator,
                zone: zone}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The presets
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The formats a program actually writes, each named after the document that defines it so that a
   reader can check one against the other.
-}

-- `2026-08-29T12:00:00`, which is ISO 8601's extended form with no zone - what a log line wants.
pub fn iso8601(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = DateFormat {value: value}

-- `2026-08-29T12:00:00Z`, or `+02:00` where the date carries an offset. RFC 3339 §5.6.
pub fn rfc3339(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = DateFormat {value: value, zone: ZoneZulu}

-- `20260829T120000Z` - the same instant with every separator dropped, which is ISO 8601's basic
-- form and what a file name wants.
pub fn iso8601Compact(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat =
    DateFormat {value: value, dateSeparator: 0, timeSeparator: 0, zone: ZoneZulu}

-- `Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:00:00 GMT` - RFC 7231 §7.1.1.1's one legal HTTP date, which is RFC 5322's
-- with the zone fixed and the day always two digits.
pub fn httpDate(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat =
    DateFormat {value: value, order: DayMonthYear, dateSeparator: 32, month: MonthAbbrev,
                weekday: WeekdayAbbrev, between: 32, zone: ZoneUtcName}

-- `2026-08-29` and `12:00:00`, which are the two halves asked for on their own often enough to name.
pub fn dateOnly(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = DateFormat {value: value, time: NoTime}

pub fn timeOnly(value: 'Date) -> DateFormat = DateFormat {value: value, order: NoDate}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Writing one
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-}

-- The English month names, as one function rather than a table: `nameOf` generates exactly this for
-- an enum and a month is a number here rather than a constructor, so the chain is written out. It
-- folds to one string wherever the month is known and is a jump table where it is not.
fn monthName(month: Int) -> String = match month:
    1 -> "January"
    2 -> "February"
    3 -> "March"
    4 -> "April"
    5 -> "May"
    6 -> "June"
    7 -> "July"
    8 -> "August"
    9 -> "September"
    10 -> "October"
    11 -> "November"
    12 -> "December"
    _ -> "?"

-- Three units of the name above, which is what every abbreviation in use is - and is why this is a
-- slice of the one table rather than a second one to keep in step with it.
fn monthAbbrev(month: Int) -> String = String.slice(monthName(month), 0, 3)

fn weekdayName(day: Weekday) -> String = nameOf(day)
fn weekdayAbbrev(day: Weekday) -> String = String.slice(nameOf(day), 0, 3)

-- A field at a fixed width, which every part of a date but the year is. `number` is
-- `Core/Format/Number.yana`'s, so the zero padding is one setting rather than a branch here.
--
-- At `Long` and not at `Int`, because the parts arrive at the widths `Date` stores them in - a `U8`
-- hour, a `U32` micros - and `U32` does not fit an `I32`. One widening rung serves all of them,
-- where a parameter at each part's own type would be six bodies saying the same line.
fn pushFixed(value: Long, digits: Size, &to: String) -> {} =
    show(number(value, digits: digits), to)

-- The year, which is the one part whose width is not fixed and the one that can be negative. Four
-- digits is ISO 8601's minimum and a year outside it simply takes the room it needs.
fn pushYear(year: I32, &to: String) -> {} = show(number(year, digits: 4), to)

fn pushSeparator(unit: CodeUnit, &to: String):
    if unit != 0 then String.pushUnit(to, unit :: Int)

{-
   The three parts a date is made of, and the order a setting puts them in.

   One `match` answering *which* parts, and one body writing a part - rather than three arms each
   transcribing the same three writes and the two separators between them. The orders differ in
   nothing but sequence, so sequence is the only thing worth saying three times.
-}
data DatePart = @value(0) YearPart | @value(1) MonthPart | @value(2) DayPart

fn datePartsOf(order: DateOrder) -> {DatePart, DatePart, DatePart} = match order:
    NoDate -> {YearPart, MonthPart, DayPart}
    YearMonthDay -> {YearPart, MonthPart, DayPart}
    DayMonthYear -> {DayPart, MonthPart, YearPart}
    MonthDayYear -> {MonthPart, DayPart, YearPart}

fn pushDatePiece(value: DateFormat, part: DatePart, &to: String):
    let date = value.value :: Date

    match part:
        YearPart: pushYear(date.year, to)
        MonthPart: pushMonth(value, to)
        DayPart: pushFixed(widen(date.day) :: Long, 2, to)

fn pushDatePart(value: DateFormat, &to: String):
    if value.order is NoDate then return

    let {first, second, third} = datePartsOf(value.order)

    pushDatePiece(value, first, to)
    pushSeparator(value.dateSeparator, to)
    pushDatePiece(value, second, to)
    pushSeparator(value.dateSeparator, to)
    pushDatePiece(value, third, to)

fn pushMonth(value: DateFormat, &to: String):
    let date = value.value :: Date
    let month = date.month :: Int

    match value.month:
        MonthNumber: pushFixed(widen(month) :: Long, 2, to)
        MonthName: String.push(to, monthName(month))
        MonthAbbrev: String.push(to, monthAbbrev(month))

fn pushTimePart(value: DateFormat, &to: String):
    let date = value.value :: Date
    if value.time is NoTime then return

    pushFixed(widen(date.hour) :: Long, 2, to)
    pushSeparator(value.timeSeparator, to)
    pushFixed(widen(date.minute) :: Long, 2, to)

    if value.time is Minutes then return

    pushSeparator(value.timeSeparator, to)
    pushFixed(widen(date.second) :: Long, 2, to)

    -- The fraction, whose separator is a point in every format that has one.
    match value.time:
        Millis:
            String.pushUnit(to, 46)
            pushFixed((widen(date.micros) :: Long) / 1000, 3, to)

        Micros:
            String.pushUnit(to, 46)
            pushFixed(widen(date.micros) :: Long, 6, to)

        _: {}

fn pushZonePart(value: DateFormat, &to: String):
    let date = value.value :: Date
    let minutes = date.zoneMinutes :: Int

    match value.zone:
        NoZone: {}

        ZoneUtcName: String.push(to, " GMT")

        ZoneZulu:
            if minutes == 0 then String.pushUnit(to, 90)
            else pushOffset(minutes, True, to)

        ZoneOffset: pushOffset(minutes, False, to)
        ZoneOffsetColon: pushOffset(minutes, True, to)

-- `+02:00` or `-0530`. The sign is the offset's own and is written for zero too, which is what every
-- format that asks for an offset rather than for `Z` expects.
fn pushOffset(minutes: Int, colon: Bool, &to: String):
    if minutes < 0 then String.pushUnit(to, 45) else String.pushUnit(to, 43)

    let size = if minutes < 0 then -minutes else minutes
    pushFixed((widen(size) :: Long) / 60, 2, to)
    if colon then String.pushUnit(to, 58)
    pushFixed((widen(size) :: Long) % 60, 2, to)

instance Show(DateFormat):
    fn show(value: DateFormat, &to: String):
        let date = value.value :: Date

        -- The weekday comes off the date rather than out of it, which is why it is not a field of
        -- `Date`: it is a function of the other three and storing it would be a fourth thing to keep
        -- in step. `weekdayOf` is that function.
        match value.weekday:
            NoWeekday: {}

            WeekdayName:
                String.push(to, weekdayName(weekdayOf(date)))
                String.push(to, ", ")

            WeekdayAbbrev:
                String.push(to, weekdayAbbrev(weekdayOf(date)))
                String.push(to, ", ")

        pushDatePart(value, to)

        -- The unit between the two halves is written only where there are two halves to put it
        -- between, which is what makes `dateOnly` and `timeOnly` the same record as the rest.
        if !(value.order is NoDate) && !(value.time is NoTime) then pushSeparator(value.between, to)

        pushTimePart(value, to)
        pushZonePart(value, to)

    {-
       The bound, which is generous and constant.

       Every part has a width the settings fix, and the widest reading of each is what this adds up:
       a weekday name and its comma and space (11), a year (11 - `Show(I32)`'s own bound), two
       separators (2), a month name (9), a day (2), the unit between (1), a time with microseconds
       (15), and a zone (6). That is 57, rounded up to 64 so that no future segment silently makes it
       wrong.

       A constant rather than a sum over the settings, deliberately. The settings are known at every
       call site that uses a preset, so a computed bound would fold to a number anyway - and what it
       would cost is a `showBound` that has to be kept in step with `show` field by field. Sixty-four
       units is one cache line and less than any date's text is worth arguing about.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: DateFormat) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(64)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `IntervalFormat` - a duration as a human reads one
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Interval)` writes the microseconds, which is the right default for the same reason
   `Show(Timestamp)` is and is unreadable at any scale above a millisecond. This writes `1h23m4.5s`,
   dropping the parts that are zero from the *left*, so the units present are the ones the duration
   actually has.

   **The largest unit is a day and the smallest a microsecond.** Above a day the answer stops being
   arithmetic - a month is not a fixed number of them - and an `Interval` is a duration rather than a
   span between two dates, so a month is a thing it cannot mean.
-}
pub data IntervalFormat {
    value: 'Interval,
    decimals: Size = 0
}

-- `decimals` is the fraction of a second, which is what a duration is read to: `0` writes `1h23m4s`
-- and `3` writes `1h23m4.500s`.
pub fn duration(value: 'Interval, decimals: Size = 0) -> IntervalFormat =
    IntervalFormat {value: value, decimals: decimals}

instance Show(IntervalFormat):
    fn show(value: IntervalFormat, &to: String):
        let total = toMicros(value.value)
        let size = if total < 0 then -total else total

        if total < 0 then String.pushUnit(to, 45)

        let days = size / 86400000000
        let hours = (size / 3600000000) % 24
        let minutes = (size / 60000000) % 60
        let seconds = (size / 1000000) % 60
        let micros = size % 1000000

        -- Each unit is written once something above it has been, so the leading zeros go and the
        -- ones inside stay: `1h0m4s` keeps its minutes because its hours were written.
        let &started = False

        if days != 0:
            show(days, to)
            String.pushUnit(to, 100)
            started = True

        if started || hours != 0:
            show(hours, to)
            String.pushUnit(to, 104)
            started = True

        if started || minutes != 0:
            show(minutes, to)
            String.pushUnit(to, 109)

        show(seconds, to)

        if value.decimals > 0:
            String.pushUnit(to, 46)

            -- The fraction, truncated to the places asked for rather than rounded: a duration that
            -- reads as shorter than it is, is the direction a reader can act on.
            let &scaled = micros
            let &left = 6 - (widen(value.decimals) :: Long)

            while left > 0:
                scaled = scaled / 10
                left = left - 1

            show(number(scaled, digits: value.decimals), to)

        String.pushUnit(to, 115)

    -- Twenty for the days, two units apiece for the three unit letters and their numbers, a point
    -- and the places after it. Generous, and constant for the same reason `DateFormat`'s is.
    fn showBound(value: IntervalFormat) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(40 + value.decimals)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Reading a date back - the other direction of everything above.
   ==========================================================================================

   The dual of `dateFormat` argument for argument: the same nine settings say what the text looks
   like, and what comes back is an owned `Date` rather than a formatter over one. A reader has no
   subject to borrow, which is the whole difference between the two directions - see the same
   paragraph in `Core/Format/Number.yana`, whose readers these are written on the model of.

   **This is a reader for the shapes this file writes, and not a date parser.** `readIso8601` reads
   what `iso8601` wrote; `readDate` with settings reads what `dateFormat` with those settings wrote.
   A text of unknown shape is a different problem - it needs a grammar and a list of formats to try -
   and a caller who has one can try these in turn, since a failed read leaves the cursor where it
   was.

   **Where it is lenient, it is lenient on purpose and in one direction only.** A field written with
   a separator between its parts may be read with fewer digits than were written (`2026-8-9`), since
   nothing is ambiguous there; a compact format has no separators, so its widths are exact and a
   short field is an error. A zone is accepted in any of the spellings this file writes whichever one
   was asked for, because they are distinguishable by their first unit and refusing `Z` where `+0000`
   was expected would be pedantry rather than checking.

   **A negative year is refused**, which is the one thing the writer can produce and this cannot: the
   sign and the date separator are the same unit, so `-2026-08-29` has two readings and neither of
   them is more right than the other. Years before 1 AD are outside every format here in practice.
-}

-- Up to `digits` decimal digits, or exactly that many when `exact` - which is what a format with no
-- separators needs, since nothing else says where a field ends. Nothing is consumed on a failure.
fn readFieldDigits(text: String, &at: Size, digits: Size, exact: Bool) -> Result(ReadError, Long):
    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at
    let &value = 0 :: Long
    let &seen = 0 :: Size

    while seen < digits && cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(text, cursor)):
        value = value * 10 + (widen(String.unit(text, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
        cursor = cursor + 1
        seen = seen + 1

    if seen == 0 then return Err(NoDigits)
    if exact && seen != digits then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(value)

-- A separator the format says is there. A zero unit is a format that writes none, so there is
-- nothing to expect and nothing to consume.
fn expectUnit(text: String, &at: Size, unit: CodeUnit) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if unit == 0 then return Ok({})
    if at >= length(text) || String.unit(text, at) != (unit :: Int) then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = at + 1
    return Ok({})

-- Whether `word` stands at this position, compared without case - which is what lets `JUL` and `jul`
-- read as the month `Jul` was written as.
fn matchesAt(text: String, at: Size, word: String) -> Bool:
    let units = length(word)
    if at + units > length(text) then return False

    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < units:
        if toLowerUnit(String.unit(text, at + i)) != toLowerUnit(String.unit(word, i)) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   The month, in whichever of the three spellings the format writes.

   The two name forms are a search over the twelve rather than a table lookup, because the names are
   what `monthName` above already answers and a second list here would be a second thing to keep in
   step. Twelve comparisons on a date parse is nothing worth arranging around, and the full name is
   tried before the abbreviation so that `June` does not read as `Jun` with an `e` left over.
-}
fn readMonthField(text: String, &at: Size, style: MonthStyle, separator: CodeUnit) -> Result(ReadError, Long):
    match style:
        MonthNumber: return readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)

        _:
            let &month = 1 :: Int

            while month <= 12:
                let full = monthName(month)
                let short = monthAbbrev(month)

                if matchesAt(text, at, full):
                    at = at + length(full)
                    return Ok(widen(month) :: Long)

                if matchesAt(text, at, short):
                    at = at + length(short)
                    return Ok(widen(month) :: Long)

                month = month + 1

            return Err(NoDigits)

-- The weekday a format writes in front of the date, which is a function of the date itself - so it
-- is read past rather than read: whatever it says, the date decides what day it was. The comma and
-- the space after it go with it.
fn skipWeekday(text: String, &at: Size):
    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at

    while cursor < units && isAlpha(String.unit(text, cursor)):
        cursor = cursor + 1

    if cursor == at then return

    while cursor < units && (String.unit(text, cursor) == 44 || String.unit(text, cursor) == 32):
        cursor = cursor + 1

    at = cursor

{-
   The zone, in any of the spellings this file writes.

   `Z`, `GMT` and `UTC` are zero; `+HH:MM` and `+HHMM` are their minutes, and the colon is optional
   in both directions. A leading space is consumed because `ZoneUtcName` writes one - and consuming
   it before a `+` costs nothing, since no format here writes a space before an offset.
-}
fn readZoneField(text: String, &at: Size, style: ZoneStyle) -> Result(ReadError, I16):
    if style is NoZone then return Ok(0)

    let units = length(text)
    let &cursor = at

    if cursor < units && String.unit(text, cursor) == 32 then cursor = cursor + 1
    if cursor >= units then return Err(NoDigits)

    let unit = toLowerUnit(String.unit(text, cursor))

    if unit == 122:
        at = cursor + 1
        return Ok(0)

    if matchesAt(text, cursor, "gmt") || matchesAt(text, cursor, "utc"):
        at = cursor + 3
        return Ok(0)

    if unit != 43 && unit != 45 then return Err(NoDigits)

    let negative = unit == 45
    cursor = cursor + 1

    let hours = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 2, True)?
    if cursor < units && String.unit(text, cursor) == 58 then cursor = cursor + 1
    let minutes = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 2, True)?

    let total = hours * 60 + minutes
    if total > 1440 then return Err(OutOfRange)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(truncate(if negative then -total else total) :: I16)

-- One of the three date pieces, written into the date being built. The ranges are checked here
-- rather than left to `timestampOf`, which deliberately accepts month 13 as January of the next
-- year: that is right for arithmetic and wrong for an input, where a number outside the calendar is
-- the input's mistake and `OutOfRange` is what says so.
fn readDatePiece(text: String, &at: Size, part: DatePart, style: MonthStyle,
                 separator: CodeUnit, &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    match part:
        YearPart:
            let year = readFieldDigits(text, at, if separator == 0 then 4 else 9, separator == 0)?
            date.year = truncate(year) :: I32

        MonthPart:
            let month = readMonthField(text, at, style, separator)?
            if month < 1 || month > 12 then return Err(OutOfRange)
            date.month = truncate(month) :: U8

        DayPart:
            let day = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
            if day < 1 || day > 31 then return Err(OutOfRange)
            date.day = truncate(day) :: U8

    return Ok({})

{-
   The time, to the precision the format says it was written at.

   Read as the writer writes it, step for step: an hour and a minute, then a second where the detail
   asks for one, then a fraction where it asks for that. The fraction is read as up to six digits and
   scaled to microseconds by how many arrived, so `.5` is half a second and `.500` is the same
   number - which is what makes a `Millis` format and a `Micros` one readable by one body.

   A second of 60 is accepted, which is the one place this is looser than the writer: a leap second
   is written by other people's formatters and refusing to read one would be refusing a real input.
-}
fn readTimePart(text: String, &at: Size, detail: TimeDetail, separator: CodeUnit,
                &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if detail is NoTime then return Ok({})

    let hour = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
    expectUnit(text, at, separator)?
    let minute = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?

    if hour > 23 || minute > 59 then return Err(OutOfRange)

    date.hour = truncate(hour) :: U8
    date.minute = truncate(minute) :: U8

    if detail is Minutes then return Ok({})

    expectUnit(text, at, separator)?
    let second = readFieldDigits(text, at, 2, separator == 0)?
    if second > 60 then return Err(OutOfRange)
    date.second = truncate(second) :: U8

    -- The fraction is read wherever there are seconds to attach it to, and not only where the
    -- setting asks for one - which is the second place this is looser than the writer and is worth
    -- the difference: `Seconds` does not write a fraction, and a timestamp another system wrote at
    -- millisecond precision is one this should read rather than stop in the middle of.
    readFraction(text, at, date)?
    return Ok({})

-- The sub-second part, whose separator is a point in every format that has one. Absent is not an
-- error: a whole number of seconds is a legal reading of a format that admits a fraction.
fn readFraction(text: String, &at: Size, &date: Date) -> Result(ReadError, {}):
    if at >= length(text) || String.unit(text, at) != 46 then return Ok({})

    let &cursor = at + 1
    let digits = readFieldDigits(text, cursor, 6, False)?
    let seen = cursor - (at + 1)

    let &micros = digits
    let &left = 6 - seen

    while left > 0:
        micros = micros * 10
        left = left - 1

    date.micros = truncate(micros) :: U32
    at = cursor
    return Ok({})

{-
   A date in the format the settings describe - `dateFormat`'s arguments, read rather than written.

   The cursor advances only on success, so a caller may try `readRfc3339` and then `readHttpDate`
   against the same position and lose nothing by the first one failing. That is `Core/Read.yana`'s
   contract and every reader in this file keeps it.
-}
pub fn readDate(from: String, &at: Size, order: DateOrder = YearMonthDay, dateSeparator: CodeUnit = 45,
                month: MonthStyle = MonthNumber, weekday: WeekdayStyle = NoWeekday,
                between: CodeUnit = 84, time: TimeDetail = Seconds,
                timeSeparator: CodeUnit = 58, zone: ZoneStyle = NoZone) -> Result(ReadError, Date):
    let &cursor = at
    let &date = Date {year: 1970, month: 1, day: 1}

    if !(weekday is NoWeekday) then skipWeekday(from, cursor)

    if !(order is NoDate):
        let {first, second, third} = datePartsOf(order)

        readDatePiece(from, cursor, first, month, dateSeparator, date)?
        expectUnit(from, cursor, dateSeparator)?
        readDatePiece(from, cursor, second, month, dateSeparator, date)?
        expectUnit(from, cursor, dateSeparator)?
        readDatePiece(from, cursor, third, month, dateSeparator, date)?

    if !(order is NoDate) && !(time is NoTime) then expectUnit(from, cursor, between)?

    readTimePart(from, cursor, time, timeSeparator, date)?
    date.zoneMinutes = readZoneField(from, cursor, zone)?

    at = cursor
    return Ok(date)

-- The presets, read. Each one takes the settings its writer above takes, so a round trip through the
-- pair is the identity on every date either of them can name.
pub fn readIso8601(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) = readDate(from, at)

pub fn readRfc3339(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readDate(from, at, zone: ZoneZulu)

pub fn readIso8601Compact(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readDate(from, at, dateSeparator: 0, timeSeparator: 0, zone: ZoneZulu)

pub fn readHttpDate(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readDate(from, at, order: DayMonthYear, dateSeparator: 32, month: MonthAbbrev,
             weekday: WeekdayAbbrev, between: 32, zone: ZoneUtcName)

pub fn readDateOnly(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readDate(from, at, time: NoTime)

pub fn readTimeOnly(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) =
    readDate(from, at, order: NoDate)

{-
   And the pair with no settings at all, which is what a `Date` in a format hole and a `read` at a
   `Date` mean.

   ISO 8601 is the notation, because it is the one every other system accepts and the only one that
   sorts as text in the same order as the dates it names. A program that wants any other format asks
   for it by name - `print("{httpDate(d)}")` - and one that wants this one need not.
-}
instance Show(Date):
    fn show(value: Date, &to: String) -> {} = show(iso8601(value), to)
    fn showBound(value: Date) -> Maybe(Size) = showBound(iso8601(value))

instance Read(Date):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Date) = readIso8601(from, at)

{-
   A duration, read as `duration` writes it - `1h23m4.500s`.

   Each component is a number and the unit letter that says what it counts, largest first, and a
   component may be missing: `4s` and `1h0m4s` are both readings of durations this writes, and so is
   `90m`, which nothing here writes and which is what a human types.

   The fraction belongs to the seconds and is scaled by how many digits arrived, exactly as a date's
   sub-second part is - so `4.5s` is four and a half seconds whether the writer was asked for one
   decimal or three.
-}
pub fn readDuration(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Interval):
    let units = length(from)
    let &cursor = at
    let &total = 0 :: I64
    let &any = False

    let &negative = False
    if cursor < units && String.unit(from, cursor) == 45:
        negative = True
        cursor = cursor + 1

    while cursor < units:
        let &value = 0 :: Long
        let &seen = 0 :: Size

        while cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(from, cursor)) && seen < 18:
            value = value * 10 + (widen(String.unit(from, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
            cursor = cursor + 1
            seen = seen + 1

        if seen == 0 then break

        -- The fraction, which only the seconds have and which is read before the unit letter it
        -- belongs to - so `4.5s` is one component rather than two.
        let &micros = 0 :: I64

        if cursor < units && String.unit(from, cursor) == 46:
            cursor = cursor + 1
            let &places = 0 :: Size

            while cursor < units && isDigit(String.unit(from, cursor)) && places < 6:
                micros = micros * 10 + (widen(String.unit(from, cursor) - 48) :: Long)
                cursor = cursor + 1
                places = places + 1

            let &left = 6 - places
            while left > 0:
                micros = micros * 10
                left = left - 1

        if cursor >= units then return Err(NoDigits)

        let unit = toLowerUnit(String.unit(from, cursor))
        cursor = cursor + 1
        any = True

        match unit:
            100 -> total = total + value * 86400000000
            104 -> total = total + value * 3600000000
            109 -> total = total + value * 60000000
            115 -> total = total + value * 1000000 + micros
            _ -> return Err(NoDigits)

    if !any then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = cursor
    return Ok(micros(if negative then -total else total))
